  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Line Diagram",
    "overview": "Tripboard draws a trip the way Beck and Vignelli drew a metro: each day is a colored line, each plan a station on it, and the whole thing hangs off a black subway-sign band that carries the trip's name in white. The field is white paper with near-black ink. Hairline seams divide regions instead of shadows or tinted cards, and color is flat, never modulated. Every hue on the page is meaningful. The Okabe-Ito inks belong to days. Ink belongs to text, selection and flights. Red is kept for Delete.\n\nThe layout runs full-bleed. The itinerary reads as a timetable column next to a line with stations. On large screens a sticky route diagram fills the other half at full viewport height and takes the bigger share as the screen widens. Density is high but regular: every day block has the same structure and the same scale, so a nine-day trip scans like a printed line map. Hierarchy comes from scale contrast within one family, Archivo, set condensed for names and with tabular figures for times and money.\n\nOpen, not designed yet: dark mode (PRD UX-5, P1). The route diagram is a schematic stand-in. The real map (milestone M4) will replace it, and the stations, day inks and selection treatment described here should carry over to it.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "White field, near-black ink, 1px hairline seams, no gradients.",
      "One Okabe-Ito ink per day, shown as a round numbered route bullet with a 4px line running through its stations.",
      "A black signage band for the trip or brand name, with white text and a white primary button on it.",
      "Archivo throughout: semibold condensed for names, tabular figures for numbers.",
      "Selection is a filled ink station plus a 2px ink outline. Flights are heavier ink interchange rings."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Lines Own the Color Rule",
        "body": "Chromatic color appears only as a day's ink: bullets, line segments, station rims and route rows. Interface chrome (buttons, badges, meter, selection) stays ink, grey and paper. The only other hue is the text-selection tint, 22% day-1 blue.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Never Alone Rule",
        "body": "A day's ink always comes with its number in the bullet, and a station's state comes with a text badge. Color never carries meaning by itself.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Condensed Means Name Rule",
        "body": "Condensed width is for proper names (trip, day, station, route) and money figures. Running text, controls and labels stay at normal width.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Clock Rule",
